<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gulf of Sirte</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gulf of Sidra, Syrtis Major</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gulf of Sirte is a large gulf of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, between Tripolitania and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a>, known in antiquity as the Greater Syrtis. Its shallow waters and shifting sands were feared by ancient sailors, and its desert shore long divided the Punic and Greek worlds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. Nomadic peoples such as the Austuriani lived in its hinterland.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gulf-of-sirte.jpg" alt="Gulf of Sirte" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jesse Allen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gulf_of_Sirte_tmo_2007238_lrg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Gulf of Sirte, also called the Gulf of Sidra, lies on the northern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> and takes its name from the oil port of Sidra or from the city of Sirte on its western coast. In antiquity it was known as the Great Sirte or Greater Syrtis, in Latin Syrtis Major, in contrast to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-gabes">Syrtis Minor</a> on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. It measures 439 kilometres from the promontory of Boreum, now Ras Teyonas, in the east to the promontory of Cephalae, Ras Kasr Hamet, in the west, reaches 177 kilometres inland and covers about 57,000 square kilometres. It has been a major centre of tuna fishing in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> for centuries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ancient writers regarded the Syrtes as notorious sandbanks that sailors took pains to avoid. The greater Syrtis is unusually tidal, with a strong clockwise current at the rising tide, and the landward side was a featureless plain, in contrast to the fertile land of Tripolitania to the west. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> describes the march of the Roman general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cato-the-younger">Cato the Younger</a> in 47 BCE as thirty days through deep and scorching sand. The reputation also appears in Roman poetry, for example in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catullus">Catullus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a>.</p><p>These sources should not be taken at face value. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomponius-mela">Pomponius Mela</a> claimed that the greater gulf had no ports, but Pseudo-Scylax, writing in the early 4th century BCE, records one. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> tells of Cato's march more calmly than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, saying that it took seven days and that locals were engaged to protect the troops from serpents. Strabo himself says that sailors kept to the coast at a distance and took care not to be driven into the gulfs by winds, so they took precautions rather than avoided the area.</p><p>Two naval battles between the Regia Marina and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> were fought in the gulf in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, in December 1941 and March 1942. In 1973 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muammar-gaddafi">Muammar Gaddafi</a> claimed much of the gulf as Libyan internal waters, drawing a line that he called the Line of Death, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> held naval exercises there to assert that these were international waters. American fighters shot down Libyan aircraft in the gulf in 1981 and 1989.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gulf of Sidra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They lived in the desert hinterland of the <b>Gulf of Sirte</b> and first appear in the 360s, when they raided the territory of Leptis Magna after one of their men, Stachao, was burned alive by the Romans.</blockquote><a href="/state/austuriani">Austuriani</a></li><li><blockquote>Herodotus, in the 5th century BCE, places them on the shores of the <b>Greater Syrtis</b>, after they took over the lands of the Psylli.</blockquote><a href="/state/nasamones">Nasamones</a></li><li><blockquote>Its capital was Leptis Magna, and the province stretched from the Gulf of Gabès to the <b>Gulf of Sirte</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/tripolitania">Tripolitania</a></li></ul></section>
